### When do you use each executor?

Java provides four ready-made executor shapes, each designed for a specific workload pattern.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ExecutorChoiceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Fixed: steady stream of trade settlements
        ExecutorService fixed = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(16);

        // Cached: burst of SMS notifications
        ExecutorService cached = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();

        // Scheduled: daily interest posting at 2 AM
        ScheduledExecutorService scheduled = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(2);
        scheduled.scheduleAtFixedRate(
                () -> System.out.println("Posting interest"),
                0, 24, TimeUnit.HOURS);

        // ForkJoinPool: sum a million transaction amounts
        ForkJoinPool fjp = new ForkJoinPool();
    }
}
```

### Why is ForkJoinPool poor for blocking IO?

ForkJoinPool is sized to roughly one thread per core, assuming threads are always running. If a worker blocks on a database or HTTP call, that core sits idle. Other workers cannot steal from the blocked worker because its deque has no tasks to steal. The pool does not automatically grow to compensate (unless ManagedBlocker is used, which is unreliable for bulk IO).

### How do the four executors compare?

| Executor | Best for | Danger | Production advice |
|---|---|---|---|
| Fixed | Steady bounded load | Unbounded queue can OOM | Use ThreadPoolExecutor with bounded queue |
| Cached | Short bursty tasks | Unbounded threads for slow tasks | Never for blocking/slow tasks |
| Scheduled | Timed/periodic jobs | Unbounded queue | Fine for most scheduling uses |
| ForkJoinPool | CPU-bound recursion | IO blocks all workers | Never for blocking IO |

### Why are Executors factory methods dangerous in production?

The convenience factory methods hide dangerous defaults. `newFixedThreadPool` uses an unbounded `LinkedBlockingQueue` that can grow until OutOfMemoryError. `newCachedThreadPool` creates unlimited threads for slow tasks.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class SafePoolCreation {
    // WRONG: Executors.newFixedThreadPool(16) uses unbounded queue
    // CORRECT: explicit bounded queue and rejection policy
    private static final ThreadPoolExecutor pool = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
        16, 16, 0L, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS,
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(500),  // bounded queue
        new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy()  // backpressure
    );
}
```

### What is the difference between scheduleAtFixedRate and scheduleWithFixedDelay?

`scheduleAtFixedRate`: runs every N time units measured from the start of each run. If a run takes longer than N, the next run starts immediately after (runs can bunch up but never overlap). `scheduleWithFixedDelay`: waits N time units after each run completes before starting the next. Runs never overlap and have a guaranteed gap between them.

```java
package com.bank.scheduling;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ScheduleDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ScheduledExecutorService scheduler = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(2);

        // Fixed rate: run every 10 seconds regardless of execution time
        // If the task takes 3s, next run starts at 10s, 20s, 30s...
        // If the task takes 12s, next run starts immediately after (at 12s, 22s...)
        scheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(() -> postInterest(), 0, 10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

        // Fixed delay: wait 10 seconds after each completion
        // If the task takes 3s, next run starts at 13s, 26s, 39s...
        // Guaranteed 10s gap between completion and next start
        scheduler.scheduleWithFixedDelay(() -> reconcile(), 0, 10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}
```

### What about the common ForkJoinPool?

The common pool (`ForkJoinPool.commonPool()`) is shared across the entire JVM by parallel streams, default CompletableFutures, and many frameworks. Blocking IO on it stalls everything. You can adjust its size with the system property `-Djava.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool.common.parallelism=N`, but this is a tuning aid, not a substitute for keeping IO off the common pool.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Should I ever use Executors factory methods in production? Prefer ThreadPoolExecutor directly for fixed pools with bounded queues. Scheduled is acceptable as-is for most use cases.
- How do virtual threads change executor choice? Virtual threads with `Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` replace cached and fixed pools for IO-bound work, since blocking is effectively free.
- Can you run a parallel stream on a custom ForkJoinPool? Yes, submit the stream operation as a task: `customPool.submit(() -> stream.parallel().map(...).collect(...)).join()`.
